  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How many termites live in a mound?",
      "a": "A large Macrotermes mound can house millions of termites. Most are workers and soldiers, all children of a single queen who can lay thousands of eggs a day in a royal chamber deep in the nest.",
      "asks": [
        "Who lives in a termite mound?",
        "How big is a termite queen?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What do termites grow inside their mounds?",
      "a": "They farm a fungus on beds of chewed plant material. The fungus breaks down tough grass and wood into food the termites can digest, a bit like a shared stomach outside their bodies.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do termites farm fungus?",
        "What is a fungus garden?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do termite mounds have chimneys?",
      "a": "The chimney and the channels near the surface let air circulate. Because the outer channels heat and cool faster than the chimney each day, the air flows one way by day and the other way at night.",
      "asks": [
        "How does air move in a termite mound?",
        "How do termite mounds stay cool?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do termite mounds breathe?",
      "a": "The mud walls are full of tiny pores. As air circulates close to the surface, carbon dioxide from the nest seeps out and fresh oxygen seeps in, much as gases pass through our lungs.",
      "asks": [
        "Why are termite mound walls porous?",
        "How do termites get fresh air?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Have buildings copied termite mounds?",
      "a": "Yes. The Eastgate Centre in Harare, Zimbabwe, was designed with chimneys and air channels inspired by termite mounds, so it stays cool with very little air conditioning.",
      "asks": [
        "What building was inspired by termites?",
        "What is the Eastgate Centre?"
      ]
    }
